-- title:
--   Trivial multidegree splits a degree-zero divisor across charts
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be an algebraically closed field, $A\subseteq L$ a valuation subring with residue field $\kappa$, and $\pi\neq 0$ an element of the maximal ideal of $A$; let $F/L$ be a field extension admitting principal divisors (every $f\neq0$ has a degree-zero divisor recording its orders at all places of $F/L$). Fix $n,m\in\mathbb N$; for each $i<n$ a field $\bar F_i$ over $\kappa$ admitting principal divisors and all of whose places over $\kappa$ are rational (the structure map to the residue field is onto), together with a `ComponentChart` $C_i$ of $F$ over $A$ with residue field $\bar F_i$, all places in $C_i.\mathrm{dom}$ being rational over $L$; for each $e<m$ two annuli $An_e,An'_e$ with the same domain, the same modulus, the modulus nonzero in $L$ and equal to a unit times $\pi^{w_e}$, and with the two parameters multiplying to the modulus; indices $src\,e,tgt\,e$ and places $xs_e$ of $\bar F_{src\,e}$, $xt_e$ of $\bar F_{tgt\,e}$ with $An_e$ attached to $C_{src\,e}$ at $xs_e$ and $An'_e$ to $C_{tgt\,e}$ at $xt_e$ (so these are nodes, the parameter's residue has order $1$ there, and the stated unit condition on evaluations holds); the condition that every node of every chart is the endpoint of exactly one annulus end; and the covering condition that every place of $F/L$ lies in exactly one chart domain and no annulus domain, or in exactly one annulus domain and no chart domain. Let $V=\mathrm{Fin}\,n\sqcup\coprod_e\mathrm{Fin}(w_e-1)$, let each annulus $e$ contribute a chain of $w_e$ edges from $src\,e$ to $tgt\,e$ through its interior vertices (`ends`), and let `lap` send a vertex to the corresponding row of the graph Laplacian. Let $D$ be a divisor of degree $0$ each point of whose support lies in a chart domain or in an annulus domain with the value there of the annulus parameter a unit times $\pi^d$ in $A$ ('lattice points'). Assume given a homomorphism $\varphi$ from divisors to $(V\to\mathbb Z)$ modulo the subgroup generated by the range of `lap` which sends a point of $C_i.\mathrm{dom}$ to the class of $\delta_{\mathrm{inl}\,i}$, a lattice point of depth $d$ with $0<d<w_e$ to the class of $\delta_{\mathrm{inr}(e,d-1)}$, and a non-lattice point of an annulus to $0$, which kills every principal divisor supported on lattice points, and which satisfies the lifting hypothesis: for every unquotiented $\mu:\mathrm{Divisor}\to(V\to\mathbb Z)$ with the same three normalisations, every $c:V\to\mathbb Z$ and every divisor $Dan$ supported on annulus lattice points with $\mu(Dan)$ agreeing with $\sum_u c_u\cdot\mathrm{lap}_u$ at all interior vertices, there is a nonzero $f$ whose divisor agrees with $Dan$ on all annulus domains and whose $\mu$-image agrees with $\sum_u c_u\cdot\mathrm{lap}_u$ at all chart vertices. Then if $\varphi D=0$ there are a nonzero $f\in F$ with divisor $E$ supported on lattice points and divisors $D_i$ with $D-E=\sum_i D_i$, each $D_i$ supported in $C_i.\mathrm{dom}$ and of degree $0$.
--
--   This is the divisor-theoretic splitting step in the analysis of the divisor class group of a curve presented by a semistable covering by component charts and annuli: a degree-zero divisor supported on the lattice whose tropical multidegree class vanishes in the Laplacian quotient differs from a sum of degree-zero divisors on the individual components by the divisor of a function. The multidegree homomorphism $\varphi$ and the lifting property are taken as hypotheses rather than constructed here; the result is used in the bound on the torsion of the tropical-position-zero part of the class group for a semistable model.
